// @description Resets requests whose body is Brotli-compressed (Content-Encoding: br), the way in to the Node.js Brotli decoder bug.
// @patches Node.js before 22.23.4, 24.22.1 and 26.12.1 (20 and older are end-of-life and unpatched); Bun before its October 2026 fix
// @cve CVE-2026-111497
// @severity critical
// @tags http, nodejs, rce, brotli, memory-corruption
// @knob port  The port to guard. Streams are reassembled only on 3000; see below.
//
// Node2Shell is memory corruption in the Brotli decoder that ships inside
// Node.js. A server reaches it without ever calling Brotli itself: Express 5
// and Koa inflate a request body according to its Content-Encoding, so one
// POST with malformed Brotli is enough. Node lists no workaround. But a
// browser never compresses what it sends, and almost no client sends `br`,
// so the request can simply be refused:
//
//   node2shell_brotli  a Content-Encoding header whose whole value is `br`
//
// Checked against Express 5 and Koa on Node 24. Both hand the body to the
// Brotli decoder for `br` alone: under any case of the header name, with no
// space or with spaces and tabs around the value, after kilobytes of other
// headers, and for JSON, form and text bodies alike. Express also takes `BR`.
// Neither does for a list (gzip, br), a quoted value, a parameter (br;q=1),
// the header given twice, or a folded line; those are answered 415 or 400
// before any decoding. So the rule is exactly that one value, and gzip and
// deflate bodies are left alone.
//
// What it does not cover: Node as a client. fetch() and http clients decode
// Brotli from the servers they call, and a filter on inbound requests sees
// none of that. Nor HTTP/2, nor anything inside TLS. A framework that
// accepts some other spelling of the header was not found, but only these
// two were tried.
//
// The cost: a client that really does send Brotli-compressed request bodies
// is reset. Have it send gzip until Node is upgraded.
//
// Plaintext HTTP/1 only: put it behind the TLS terminator.
//
// Reassembly is tied to port 3000. A bare `reassemble;` follows every TCP
// connection on the interface and, when the policy is applied, resets each
// one already open, on any port. So with the `port` knob set to anything
// else, packets are judged one at a time and a signature split across two is
// missed: to guard another port properly, change the number here as well.
nocase;
reassemble 3000;
regex brotli = "\ncontent-encoding:[ \t]*br[ \t]*[\r\n]";
filter {
  if (proto != 6) decide pass;
  if (dport != knob("port") ?? 3000) decide pass;
  if (scan(payload, 4096) & brotli) decide rst("node2shell_brotli", 0);
  decide pass;
}
